### Why is GET with sensitive data in the URL dangerous?

GET request URLs are recorded and exposed in many places even when the connection is encrypted.

```text
GET /verify?aadhaar=1234-5678-9012 HTTP/1.1

Where this URL ends up:
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ 1. Web server access log: 200 GET /verify?aadhaar=.. │
│ 2. Proxy / load-balancer log                         │
│ 3. CDN log                                           │
│ 4. Application log (if request is logged)            │
│ 5. Browser history and bookmarks                     │
│ 6. HTTP Referer header (if page links to another)    │
│ 7. Browser auto-complete suggestions                 │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

TLS encrypts the URL in transit so a network eavesdropper cannot read it, but it does nothing about all the endpoint storage.

### How does POST with the body avoid the problem?

A POST places the data in the request body, which is not recorded by standard infrastructure.

```text
POST /verify HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json

{"aadhaar": "1234-5678-9012"}

Where the body does NOT end up:
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ 1. Web server access log: 200 POST /verify (no body) │
│ 2. Browser history: POST /verify (no body)            │
│ 3. Referer header: /verify (no body)                  │
│ 4. Bookmarks: not bookmarkable                        │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

### How do you implement this in a Spring Boot API?

Rajesh moves the Aadhaar from a GET parameter to a POST body at National Bank of India.

```java
package com.bank.controller;

import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/kyc")
public class KycController {

    // WRONG: Aadhaar in URL
    @GetMapping("/verify")
    public String verifyBad(@RequestParam String aadhaar) {
        // Aadhaar appears in access logs, history, referrer
        return kycService.verify(aadhaar);
    }

    // CORRECT: Aadhaar in POST body
    @PostMapping("/verify")
    public String verifyGood(@RequestBody KycRequest request) {
        // Aadhaar stays in the request body, not logged by default
        return kycService.verify(request.aadhaar());
    }

    public record KycRequest(String aadhaar) {}
}
```

### What about lookups that semantically should be GET?

Even if a read operation would normally use GET, send the sensitive identifier in a POST body or use an internal non-sensitive id.

```java
package com.bank.controller;

import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/customer")
public class CustomerController {

    // Option 1: POST body for the lookup
    @PostMapping("/search")
    public CustomerResponse searchByPan(@RequestBody PanSearchRequest request) {
        return customerService.findByPan(request.pan());
    }

    // Option 2: use internal non-sensitive ID in the URL
    @GetMapping("/{customerId}")
    public CustomerResponse getByInternalId(@PathVariable long customerId) {
        return customerService.findById(customerId);
    }

    public record PanSearchRequest(String pan) {}
}
```

### What additional defenses are needed beyond POST?

POST with the body is necessary but not sufficient. Defense in depth is required.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;

@Configuration
public class SensitiveDataDefenseChecklist {
    // 1. HTTPS everywhere (never plain HTTP)
    // 2. POST body for sensitive identifiers
    // 3. Mask Aadhaar/PAN in application logs
    //    log.info("Verifying Aadhaar: ****{}", last4Digits);
    // 4. Cache-Control: no-store on responses
    // 5. Encrypt at rest (field-level encryption or tokenization)
    // 6. Strict authorization: only authorized users can call /verify
    // 7. Input validation: Aadhaar must match format \d{4}-\d{4}-\d{4}
}
```

### How do you mask sensitive data in application logs?

Even with POST, the application might log the request body. Mask sensitive fields.

```java
package com.bank.logging;

public class SensitiveDataMasker {

    public static String maskAadhaar(String aadhaar) {
        if (aadhaar == null || aadhaar.length() < 4) return "****";
        return "****-****-" + aadhaar.substring(aadhaar.length() - 4);
    }

    public static String maskPan(String pan) {
        if (pan == null || pan.length() < 4) return "****";
        return "******" + pan.substring(pan.length() - 4);
    }
}

// Usage in service:
// log.info("Verifying Aadhaar: {}", SensitiveDataMasker.maskAadhaar(aadhaar));
// Logs: Verifying Aadhaar: ****-****-9012
```

### How do you set Cache-Control headers?

```java
package com.bank.controller;

import org.springframework.http.*;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;

@RestController
public class KycController {

    @PostMapping("/api/kyc/verify")
    public ResponseEntity<KycResult> verify(@RequestBody KycRequest request) {
        KycResult result = kycService.verify(request.aadhaar());
        return ResponseEntity.ok()
            .cacheControl(CacheControl.noStore())  // no caching!
            .body(result);
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- POST body is the minimum; field-level encryption or tokenization is the gold standard.
- Consider hashing the Aadhaar/PAN at rest and storing only the hash for lookups.
- Ensure that neither the WAF, API gateway, nor the load balancer logs the request body.
- Audit access to the /verify endpoint and alert on unusual patterns.
- OWASP guidelines recommend never passing sensitive data in URLs.
